course: dictionary
lesson: 7120
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. The word peep can mean a quick, secret look, or a short, high sound. «A stripy head peeps out of a hedge: Badger.» A head popping out for a quick look shows that peep means look quickly.
explain: «Before hatching, the chicks peep inside the shell and the hen clucks back.» The hen answers the chicks, so peep here is a small sound, like a baby bird makes. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning fits.
explain: To check your choice, put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If it still makes sense, you have found the right meaning. Here, «the chicks look quickly inside the shell» makes less sense than «the chicks make a small sound inside the shell.»
explain: Phrases change meaning too. «Not a peep» means no sound or no complaint at all, so a teacher who says «Not a peep!» wants the room silent.
fact: To peep (verb) means to look quickly and secretly, often through a small hole or gap.
fact: A peep (noun) is a quick look, or a short, high sound like a baby bird makes.
fact: «Not a peep» means no sound or no complaint at all.
fact: «Peeping Tom» means someone who spies on people, which is wrong.
fact: The verb forms are peep, peeps, peeped and peeping.
exercise: «A stripy head peeps out of a hedge.» What does peeps mean here? | *Looks quickly from a hiding place | Falls down | Grows tall | Sings loudly
exercise: «Before hatching, the chicks peep inside the shell.» What kind of peep is this? | *A short, high sound | A quick secret look | A long, slow walk | A loud shout
exercise: Dad says, «Not a peep out of you!» What does he mean? | Shout as loudly as you can | Open the window | *Be completely quiet | Tell me a joke
test: «The joey peeps out of its mother's pouch to watch.» What does peeps mean here? | Hides and never looks | Makes a loud noise | *Pokes out for a quick look | Jumps right out and runs
test: In the phrase «take a peep», what is a peep? | *A quick look | A small bird | A loud argument | A long, slow stare
exam: Mia says peep only ever means a sound. Is she right? | Yes, it always means a loud shout | *No, it can also mean to look quickly and secretly | Yes, it always means a sound | No, it only means a fast run
